LeetCode 994 — Rotting Oranges

From Brute Force → Most Optimal | All Approaches + Code + Explanation + Dry Run

1. Problem Statement

You are given a grid:

0 = Empty cell
1 = Fresh orange
2 = Rotten orange

Every minute, a rotten orange makes its 4-directionally adjacent fresh oranges rotten.

Return the minimum number of minutes required to rot all oranges.

If some fresh oranges can never become rotten, return:

-1
Example
Input:


2 1 1
1 1 0
0 1 1


Output:
4

The rotting spreads like:

Minute 0:


2 1 1
1 1 0
0 1 1
Minute 1:


2 2 1
2 1 0
0 1 1
Minute 2:


2 2 2
2 2 0
0 1 1
Minute 3:


2 2 2
2 2 0
0 2 1
Minute 4:


2 2 2
2 2 0
0 2 2

Answer:

4

Approach 1 — Brute Force Simulation
Idea

At every minute:

Scan the entire matrix.
Find all currently rotten oranges.
Rot their neighboring fresh oranges.
Repeat until no new orange becomes rotten.

The important problem is that if we simply modify the grid while scanning, newly rotten oranges might incorrectly spread in the same minute.

So we can use a temporary grid / list of newly rotten cells.

Code — Brute Force
class Solution {


    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {


        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;


        int fresh = 0;
        int time = 0;


        while (true) {


            List<int[]> rotten = new ArrayList<>();


            // Find all currently rotten oranges
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


                for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {


                    if (grid[i][j] == 2) {
                        rotten.add(new int[]{i, j});
                    }


                    if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                        fresh++;
                    }
                }
            }


            if (fresh == 0) {
                return time;
            }


            boolean changed = false;


            for (int[] cell : rotten) {


                int r = cell[0];
                int c = cell[1];


                int[] dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
                int[] dc = {0, 1, 0, -1};


                for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {


                    int nr = r + dr[k];
                    int nc = c + dc[k];


                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < m &&
                        grid[nr][nc] == 1) {


                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;
                        changed = true;
                    }
                }
            }


            if (!changed) {
                return -1;
            }


            time++;
            fresh = 0;
        }
    }
}

Complexity

Let:

N = rows × columns

In the worst case, we may perform N minutes, and every minute scans the entire matrix.

Time = O(N²)
Space = O(N)

❌ Not optimal.

Approach 2 — BFS From Every Fresh Orange

Another way is to ask:

For each fresh orange, how long does it take to reach a rotten orange?

For every fresh orange:

Run BFS.
Search for the nearest rotten orange.
Store its distance.
Take the maximum distance.

This works because BFS gives the shortest distance in an unweighted grid.

Code
class Solution {


    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {


        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;


        int answer = 0;


        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {


                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {


                    int distance = bfs(grid, i, j);


                    if (distance == -1) {
                        return -1;
                    }


                    answer = Math.max(answer, distance);
                }
            }
        }


        return answer;
    }


    private int bfs(int[][] grid, int sr, int sc) {


        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;


        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[n][m];


        q.offer(new int[]{sr, sc});
        visited[sr][sc] = true;


        int distance = 0;


        int[] dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 1, 0, -1};


        while (!q.isEmpty()) {


            int size = q.size();


            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {


                int[] cell = q.poll();


                int r = cell[0];
                int c = cell[1];


                if (grid[r][c] == 2) {
                    return distance;
                }


                for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {


                    int nr = r + dr[k];
                    int nc = c + dc[k];


                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < m &&
                        !visited[nr][nc]) {


                        visited[nr][nc] = true;


                        q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }


            distance++;
        }


        return -1;
    }
}

Complexity

There can be N fresh oranges, and each BFS can visit N cells.

Time = O(N²)
Space = O(N)

❌ Better conceptually, but still not optimal.

Approach 3 — Dynamic Programming / Distance Propagation

We can think about the answer for every cell as a distance to the nearest rotten orange.

For example:

2 1 1
1 1 0
0 1 1

We can propagate distances from already-known rotten cells.

However, unlike 542. 01 Matrix, this problem is naturally a time-based spreading problem.

A two-pass DP solution is possible with careful handling, but it is unnecessarily complicated because the spreading happens simultaneously from multiple rotten oranges.

So for this problem, the cleanest optimal approach is:

Multi-Source BFS

Code — DP Two-Pass Approach
JAVA
class Solution {

    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {

        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;

        int INF = 1000000;

        int[][] dist = new int[m][n];

        int fresh = 0;

        // Step 1: Initialize distance matrix
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {

                    // Already rotten
                    dist[i][j] = 0;

                } else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {

                    // Fresh orange
                    dist[i][j] = INF;
                    fresh++;

                } else {

                    // Empty cell
                    dist[i][j] = INF;
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Top-left → Bottom-right
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 0) {
                    continue;
                }

                // From top
                if (i > 0 && dist[i - 1][j] != INF) {
                    dist[i][j] =
                        Math.min(dist[i][j],
                                 dist[i - 1][j] + 1);
                }

                // From left
                if (j > 0 && dist[i][j - 1] != INF) {
                    dist[i][j] =
                        Math.min(dist[i][j],
                                 dist[i][j - 1] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: Bottom-right → Top-left
        for (int i = m - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 0) {
                    continue;
                }

                // From bottom
                if (i < m - 1 && dist[i + 1][j] != INF) {
                    dist[i][j] =
                        Math.min(dist[i][j],
                                 dist[i + 1][j] + 1);
                }

                // From right
                if (j < n - 1 && dist[i][j + 1] != INF) {
                    dist[i][j] =
                        Math.min(dist[i][j],
                                 dist[i][j + 1] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 4: Find answer
        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {

                    // Fresh orange cannot reach any rotten orange
                    if (dist[i][j] == INF) {
                        return -1;
                    }

                    answer = Math.max(answer, dist[i][j]);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}

Approach 4 — Multi-Source BFS ⭐ Optimal

This is the standard and best solution.

Key Observation

There can be multiple rotten oranges at time 0.

Instead of doing:

Rotten orange 1 → spread
Rotten orange 2 → spread
Rotten orange 3 → spread

we start BFS with all rotten oranges simultaneously.

2 1 1
1 1 0
0 1 1

Initial queue:

(0,0)

because (0,0) is rotten.

At the same time, if there were multiple 2s, all of them would be added to the queue.

Your Code ⭐

Your supplied code is the optimal multi-source BFS approach.

class Solution {


    class Pair {
        int row;
        int col;
        int step;


        public Pair(int row, int col, int step) {
            this.row = row;
            this.col = col;
            this.step = step;
        }
    }


    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {


        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;


        int count = 0;


        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();


        // Put all rotten oranges into queue
        // Count all fresh oranges
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {


                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {


                    q.offer(new Pair(i, j, 0));


                } else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {


                    count++;
                }
            }
        }


        int time = 0;
        int cnt = 0;


        int rc[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int nc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};


        while (!q.isEmpty()) {


            Pair p = q.poll();


            int r = p.row;
            int c = p.col;
            int s = p.step;


            time = Math.max(time, s);


            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {


                int nr = r + rc[i];
                int cn = c + nc[i];


                if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                    cn >= 0 && cn < m &&
                    grid[nr][cn] == 1) {


                    q.offer(new Pair(nr, cn, s + 1));


                    // Mark immediately
                    grid[nr][cn] = 2;


                    cnt++;
                }
            }
        }


        // Some fresh oranges could not be reached
        if (cnt != count) {
            return -1;
        }


        return time;
    }
}

