(2,1,3)
(2,2,4)

The final orange rots at time 4.

Therefore:

time = Math.max(time, s);

gives the total number of minutes.

Approach 5 — Multi-Source BFS Using Level Order ⭐ Cleaner

We don't actually need to store step inside every Pair.

The BFS queue itself already gives us the current level.

This version is often easier to explain in interviews.

class Solution {


    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {


        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;


        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();


        int fresh = 0;


        // Add all rotten oranges
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {


                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {


                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});


                } else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {


                    fresh++;
                }
            }
        }


        int time = 0;


        int[] dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 1, 0, -1};


        while (!q.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {


            int size = q.size();


            // One BFS level = one minute
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {


                int[] curr = q.poll();


                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];


                for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++) {


                    int nr = r + dr[j];
                    int nc = c + dc[j];


                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < m &&
                        grid[nr][nc] == 1) {


                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;


                        fresh--;


                        q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }


            time++;
        }


        return fresh == 0 ? time : -1;
    }
}

